</li>
<li data-start=3D"3543" data-end=3D"3608">
<p data-start=3D"3545" data-end=3D"3608">Serve signed URLs to validators vi=
a the <strong data-start=3D"3585" data-end=3D"3608">Competition synapse</st=
rong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"3610" data-end=3D"3613">
<h2 data-start=3D"3615" data-end=3D"3656">2=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3 Tiny CNN vs M=
LP =E2=80=93 which is better?</h2>
<p data-start=3D"3658" data-end=3D"3664">Given:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"3666" data-end=3D"3764">
<li data-start=3D"3666" data-end=3D"3708">
<p data-start=3D"3668" data-end=3D"3708">Input: <strong data-start=3D"3675"=
 data-end=3D"3690">3 =C3=97 64 =C3=97 64</strong> =3D 12,288 features</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3709" data-end=3D"3729">
<p data-start=3D"3711" data-end=3D"3729">Output: 21 classes</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3730" data-end=3D"3764">
<p data-start=3D"3732" data-end=3D"3764"><strong data-start=3D"3732" data-e=
nd=3D"3764">Accuracy is 95% of the score</strong></p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start=3D"3766" data-end=3D"3797">MLP option (for comparison)</h3>
<p data-start=3D"3799" data-end=3D"3809">If you do:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"3811" data-end=3D"3859">
<li data-start=3D"3811" data-end=3D"3830">
<p data-start=3D"3813" data-end=3D"3830">Flatten to 12,288</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3831" data-end=3D"3859">
<p data-start=3D"3833" data-end=3D"3859">FC 12,288 =E2=86=92 512 =E2=86=92 =
256 =E2=86=92 21</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"3861" data-end=3D"3885">Then params are roughly:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"3887" data-end=3D"3945">
<li data-start=3D"3887" data-end=3D"3908">
<p data-start=3D"3889" data-end=3D"3908">12,288 =C3=97 512 =E2=89=88 6.3M</=
p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3909" data-end=3D"3927">
<p data-start=3D"3911" data-end=3D"3927">512 =C3=97 256 =E2=89=88 131k</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"3928" data-end=3D"3945">
<p data-start=3D"3930" data-end=3D"3945">256 =C3=97 21 =E2=89=88 5k</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"3947" data-end=3D"3967">Total ~6.5M weights.</p>
<p data-start=3D"3969" data-end=3D"4129">In zk-land, that=E2=80=99s <strong=
 data-start=3D"3988" data-end=3D"3996">huge</strong>. Proof generation will=
 be slow, memory heavy, and you=E2=80=99re not even using spatial structure=
. Accuracy might be ok but with a big cost.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"4131" data-end=3D"4150">Tiny CNN option</h3>
<p data-start=3D"4152" data-end=3D"4234">If you use a small CNN with stride=
d convs and global average pooling, you can get:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4236" data-end=3D"4357">
<li data-start=3D"4236" data-end=3D"4279">
<p data-start=3D"4238" data-end=3D"4279">Much fewer parameters (tens of tho=
usands)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4280" data-end=3D"4302">
<p data-start=3D"4282" data-end=3D"4302">Much smaller circuit</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4303" data-end=3D"4357">
<p data-start=3D"4305" data-end=3D"4357">Better accuracy per parameter (use=
s image structure)</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"4359" data-end=3D"4384">So for SN2 competition 1:</p>
<blockquote data-start=3D"4386" data-end=3D"4490">
<p data-start=3D"4388" data-end=3D"4490"><strong data-start=3D"4388" data-e=
nd=3D"4428">CNN is absolutely the better option.</strong><br data-start=3D"=
4428" data-end=3D"4431">
MLP is only worth it if you want a =E2=80=9Chello world=E2=80=9D circuit.</=
p>
</blockquote>
<hr data-start=3D"4492" data-end=3D"4495">
<h2 data-start=3D"4497" data-end=3D"4558">3=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3 Concrete tiny=
 CNN architecture for SN2 Age Competition</h2>
<p data-start=3D"4560" data-end=3D"4591">Let=E2=80=99s design something tha=
t is:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"4593" data-end=3D"4682">
<li data-start=3D"4593" data-end=3D"4621">
<p data-start=3D"4595" data-end=3D"4621">Small enough to prove fast</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"4622" data-end=3D"4682">
<p data-start=3D"4624" data-end=3D"4682">Still good enough to hit strong ac=
curacy with distillation</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"4684" data-end=3D"4699">Assume PyTorch.</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"4701" data-end=3D"4722">3.1. Architecture</h3>
<p data-start=3D"4724" data-end=3D"4774">Input: <code data-start=3D"4731" d=
ata-end=3D"4748">B x 3 x 64 x 64</code><br data-start=3D"4748" data-end=3D"=
4751">
Output: <code data-start=3D"4759" data-end=3D"4767">B x 21</code> logits</p=
>
