<p data-start=3D"7988" data-end=3D"7998">But again:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"8000" data-end=3D"8085">
<li data-start=3D"8000" data-end=3D"8029">
<p data-start=3D"8002" data-end=3D"8029">Much larger parameter count</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8030" data-end=3D"8053">
<p data-start=3D"8032" data-end=3D"8053">Likely slower proving</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8054" data-end=3D"8085">
<p data-start=3D"8056" data-end=3D"8085">Worse image modeling than CNN</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"8087" data-end=3D"8134">So I=E2=80=99d only use this as a =
=E2=80=9Csmoke test=E2=80=9D circuit.</p>
<hr data-start=3D"8136" data-end=3D"8139">
<h2 data-start=3D"8141" data-end=3D"8175">5=EF=B8=8F=E2=83=A3 So, which opt=
ion is better?</h2>
<p data-start=3D"8177" data-end=3D"8203"><strong data-start=3D"8177" data-e=
nd=3D"8203">For SN2 competition 1:</strong></p>
<ul data-start=3D"8205" data-end=3D"8370">
<li data-start=3D"8205" data-end=3D"8320">
<p data-start=3D"8207" data-end=3D"8240">=E2=9C=85 <strong data-start=3D"82=
09" data-end=3D"8221">Tiny CNN</strong> is clearly better:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"8243" data-end=3D"8320">
<li data-start=3D"8243" data-end=3D"8265">
<p data-start=3D"8245" data-end=3D"8265">Way fewer parameters</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8268" data-end=3D"8299">
<p data-start=3D"8270" data-end=3D"8299">Better accuracy per parameter</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8302" data-end=3D"8320">
<p data-start=3D"8304" data-end=3D"8320">More zk-friendly</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8321" data-end=3D"8370">
<p data-start=3D"8323" data-end=3D"8370">=E2=9D=8C MLP is mainly for debugg=
ing / quick prototype</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start=3D"8372" data-end=3D"8375">
<p data-start=3D"8377" data-end=3D"8406">If you want, next step I can:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"8408" data-end=3D"8739">
<li data-start=3D"8408" data-end=3D"8603">
<p data-start=3D"8410" data-end=3D"8473">Turn this into a <strong data-star=
t=3D"8427" data-end=3D"8457">full small training script</strong> (PyTorch) =
with:</p>
<ul data-start=3D"8476" data-end=3D"8603">
<li data-start=3D"8476" data-end=3D"8529">
<p data-start=3D"8478" data-end=3D"8529">Dataset loader (you can plug your =
own dataset path)</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8532" data-end=3D"8553">
<p data-start=3D"8534" data-end=3D"8553">Distillation option</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8556" data-end=3D"8603">
<p data-start=3D"8558" data-end=3D"8603">ONNX export + a sample ezkl <code =
data-start=3D"8586" data-end=3D"8595">compile</code> command</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"8604" data-end=3D"8739">
<p data-start=3D"8606" data-end=3D"8739">Or sketch the exact <strong data-s=
tart=3D"8626" data-end=3D"8651"><code data-start=3D"8628" data-end=3D"8649"=
>age_competition_v1/</code></strong> directory with example file contents (=
fake hashes, etc.) ready to drop into your miner.</p>
</li>
</ul>
